ThreeUI is a React component catalog by Meng To, published at[threeui.com](https://threeui.com/) and on npm as` @designcodeio/threeui` . Each component brings its own animated renderer (Three.js, raw WebGL or Canvas 2D, depending on the component), so you add an animated 3D hero background by importing a component instead of building a scene, camera and materials.


You want a hero that stops the scroll, but a Three.js tutorial starts with scene graphs, lighting rigs and render loops before anything shows up on screen. This guide covers installing ThreeUI, rendering one background component behind a hero, fixing the asset-path problem, and deciding whether the extra weight is worth it on your page.


## Key Takeaways


- Install ThreeUI with` npm install @designcodeio/threeui` and import` @designcodeio/threeui/style.css` once from your app entrypoint.
- ThreeUI lists` react` and` react-dom` (18 or 19) and` three` as peer dependencies.
- Components that render a full HTML document load runtime files from root-relative URLs, so copy them from` node_modules/@designcodeio/threeui/lib-dist/assets/` or set` sourceUrl` or` assetBaseUrl` .
- Anything beyond a component’s exposed variants and controls means editing its source.
- Lazy-load ThreeUI components, skip them when` prefers-reduced-motion` is set, and use a static image when the 3D effect adds nothing.


## What Is ThreeUI and Who Is It For?


ThreeUI gives you ready-made animated sections (heroes, backgrounds, interactive elements) that you drop into an ordinary React page. You don’t need to know how a scene graph works. You pick a component, render it and set its props.


Several unrelated projects are called three-ui, including[poki-archive/three-ui](https://github.com/poki-archive/three-ui) and[nirholas/three-ui](https://github.com/nirholas/three-ui) . This guide is about Meng To’s ThreeUI from threeui.com and the[MengTo/threeui repository](https://github.com/MengTo/threeui) .


ThreeUI comes in two tiers: Community and Pro. Community components are MIT licensed and install from npm. Pro components are not on npm: you download them as source into your project with` npx @designcodeio/threeui-cli` after signing in with an active paid plan. The project is young ([Community was open-sourced in August 2026](https://x.com/MengTo/status/2090817187900780961) ), so expect the API to change.


## Install the ThreeUI Package


Install one npm package, then import its stylesheet once. The[ThreeUI installation page](https://threeui.com/installation) walks through setup. The published package lists` react` and` react-dom` from version 18 up to, but not including, 20, plus` three` from 0.149, as peer dependencies. npm 7 and later installs missing peers for you. The Pro CLI needs Node.js 20 or later.


```text
npm   install   @designcodeio/threeui
```


Where the stylesheet import goes depends on your framework:


Framework Where the CSS import goes


Vite` src/main.jsx`


Next.js App Router` app/layout.jsx`


```text
// src/main.jsx (Vite)
import   {   StrictMode   }   from   "  react  "  ;
import   {   createRoot   }   from   "  react-dom/client  "  ;
import   "  @designcodeio/threeui/style.css  "  ;
import   App   from   "  ./App  "  ;


createRoot  (  document  .  getElementById  (  "  root  "  )).  render  (
<  StrictMode  >
<  App   />
</  StrictMode  >
);
```


## Drop In One Component


To render a ThreeUI component, import it by name and place it in your JSX. The README’s example uses` AtTheHorizon` , but that component isn’t exported by the current 1.2.0 package, so this guide uses` EmeraldHorizonBackground` , an animated Three.js background that sits behind a hero section:


```text
import   {   EmeraldHorizonBackground   }   from   "  @designcodeio/threeui  "  ;


export   function   Hero  () {
return   <  EmeraldHorizonBackground   />  ;
}
```


The root import is the simplest option. The package also offers a per-component subpath, which pulls in less code while you develop:


```text
import   {   EmeraldHorizonBackground   }   from   "  @designcodeio/threeui/components/EmeraldHorizonBackground  "  ;
```


Props differ from one component to the next. Look them up on each component’s threeui.com page. For example,[Brand Orbs](https://threeui.com/ui-elements/brand-orbs/designcode) takes` variant` ,` size` ,` mode` ,` speed` and` paused` . Don’t guess props. To add an interaction without depending on any props, use state in the surrounding component:


```text
import   {   useState   }   from   "  react  "  ;


export   function   Hero  () {
const   [  show3D  ,   setShow3D  ]   =   useState  (  true  );


return   (
<  section  >
{show3D   ?   (
<  EmeraldHorizonBackground   />
)   :   (
<  img   src  =  "  /hero-fallback.jpg  "   alt  =  "  Product hero  "   />
)  }
<  button   onClick  =  {  ()   =>   setShow3D  ((  v  )   =>   !  v  )  }>
{show3D   ?   "  Show static image  "   :   "  Show 3D  "  }
</  button  >
</  section  >
);
}
```


## The ThreeUI Asset Gotcha


Some ThreeUI components render a full HTML document. They fetch their runtime files from fixed paths at the root of your site, the same paths the ThreeUI preview site uses. Landscape, for example, asks for` /landscape.html` by default. If your app doesn’t serve a file at that path, the component breaks. You can fix this in two ways. Copy the files you need from` node_modules/@designcodeio/threeui/lib-dist/assets/` into your public folder. Or, if the component has a` sourceUrl` or` assetBaseUrl` prop, point it at wherever you host the files.


```text
# Check the network tab for the exact paths the component requests,
# then mirror that layout under public/.
cp   -R   node_modules/@designcodeio/threeui/lib-dist/assets/.   public/
```


```text
// Only if the component's page lists this prop:
// <SomeComponent assetBaseUrl="/threeui/" />
```


Symptom Fix


Component area renders empty; network tab shows 404s under the root path Copy the needed assets into` public/` at matching paths


Assets live on a CDN or a sub-path Set` sourceUrl` or` assetBaseUrl` where the component exposes it


## How ThreeUI Sits With Plain Three.js


ThreeUI hides the renderer. In the Three.js-based components, the scene, camera, renderer, materials and animation loop all live inside the component. Other components use raw WebGL or Canvas 2D. The cost of that convenience is control: ThreeUI exposes each component’s variants and controls, and changing anything they don’t cover means editing the component’s source. You can read Community source in` node_modules` and on GitHub. The Pro CLI copies Pro source into your project.


If you expect to go past the exposed controls, learn the underlying library.[Building 3D animations using JavaScript and Three.js](https://blog.openreplay.com/3d-animations-with-js-and-three-js/) covers the fundamentals.


## ThreeUI Performance, Honestly


Every ThreeUI component runs a live animated canvas, which costs JavaScript, asset downloads and GPU time. Mobile GPUs feel that cost the most. Load components lazily, skip them for visitors who set` prefers-reduced-motion` , and use a static image anywhere the 3D effect adds nothing to the page.


[React.lazy](https://react.dev/reference/react/lazy) expects a default export, so map the named export:


```text
import   {   lazy  ,   Suspense  ,   useEffect  ,   useState   }   from   "  react  "  ;


const   EmeraldHorizonBackground   =   lazy  (()   =>
import  (  "  @designcodeio/threeui  "  ).  then  ((  m  )   =>   ({   default  :   m  .  EmeraldHorizonBackground   }))
);


function   useReducedMotion  () {
const   [  reduced  ,   setReduced  ]   =   useState  (  false  );
useEffect  (()   =>   {
const   mq   =   window  .  matchMedia  (  "  (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)  "  );
setReduced  (  mq  .  matches  );
const   onChange   =   (  e  )   =>   setReduced  (  e  .  matches  );
mq  .  addEventListener  (  "  change  "  ,   onChange  );
return   ()   =>   mq  .  removeEventListener  (  "  change  "  ,   onChange  );
}, []);
return   reduced  ;
}


function   hasWebGL  () {
const   canvas   =   document  .  createElement  (  "  canvas  "  );
return   Boolean  (  canvas  .  getContext  (  "  webgl2  "  )   ||   canvas  .  getContext  (  "  webgl  "  ));
}


const   fallback   =   <  img   src  =  "  /hero-fallback.jpg  "   alt  =  "  Product hero  "   />  ;


export   function   Hero  () {
const   reduced   =   useReducedMotion  ();
const   [  webgl  ,   setWebgl  ]   =   useState  (  false  );
useEffect  (()   =>   setWebgl  (  hasWebGL  ()), []);


if (  reduced   ||   !  webgl  )   return   fallback  ;
return   <  Suspense   fallback  =  {fallback}><  EmeraldHorizonBackground   /></  Suspense  >  ;
}
```


The` Hero` component above reads the[prefers-reduced-motion](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/At-rules/@media/prefers-reduced-motion) media feature through[window.matchMedia](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/matchMedia) and checks for a context with[HTMLCanvasElement.getContext](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/HTMLCanvasElement/getContext) . Some components handle reduced motion themselves. Brand Orbs, for example, has` speed` and` paused` props, and its source checks` prefers-reduced-motion` and pauses when it’s out of view. Check each component’s page for what it covers.


In Next.js, use[next/dynamic](https://nextjs.org/docs/app/guides/lazy-loading) . The` ssr: false` option only works inside a Client Component, and Next.js throws an error if you use it in a Server Component. Turning off server rendering is a safe default for canvas code that only runs in the browser:


```text
"  use client  "  ;
import   dynamic   from   "  next/dynamic  "  ;


const   EmeraldHorizonBackground   =   dynamic  (
()   =>   import  (  "  @designcodeio/threeui  "  ).  then  ((  m  )   =>   m  .  EmeraldHorizonBackground  ),
{   ssr  :   false  ,   loading  :   ()   =>   <  img   src  =  "  /hero-fallback.jpg  "   alt  =  "  Product hero  "   />   }
);


export default   function   Hero  ()   {
return   <  EmeraldHorizonBackground   />  ;
}
```


See the Next.js lazy loading guide for details. Run your build tool’s bundle analyzer to see what ThreeUI actually adds to your bundle. Lab tests only show part of the picture, though. A session replay tool such as[OpenReplay](https://openreplay.com/) lets you watch real visits, so you can spot a hero that stays blank or stutters on slower phones. If you need more control than a prebuilt component gives you, the lower-level options are covered in[WebGPU vs WebGL: Why the Industry Is Moving On](https://blog.openreplay.com/webgpu-vs-webgl-industry-moving/) .


Pin the ThreeUI package version. Per the README’s release rules, a Community release goes out when a maintainer merges the reviewed sync pull request, and removing a public component, variant or control triggers a major version.` AtTheHorizon` is an example: it shipped in 0.3.0 and is gone from the 1.x releases. Review the release notes before you upgrade.


## Conclusion


ThreeUI gets you a working 3D hero background with one npm install, one stylesheet import and one component. Budget time for serving the runtime assets, and keep in mind that it trades control for speed. Start by rendering one Community component behind a lazy boundary with a reduced-motion fallback. Then measure the bundle and test on a mid-range phone before you decide it stays on the page.


## FAQs


Can I use ThreeUI Community components in a commercial project?


Yes. ThreeUI Community application code, component code and ThreeUI-authored imagery are MIT licensed, which allows commercial use as long as you keep the license notice. Bundled open fonts remain under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, and bundled Three.js runtime files remain MIT licensed. Pro components are a separate paid product, so check the Pro terms before shipping them.


What happens if I rerun the ThreeUI Pro CLI after editing a downloaded component?


The ThreeUI Pro CLI won't replace files you've edited unless you add the --force flag, for example npx @designcodeio/threeui-cli add cross-beam --dir ./src --force. Using --force replaces your edits with the downloaded source, so commit your changes first and merge them back by hand. Run npx @designcodeio/threeui-cli --help to see the other commands and flags, including login and logout.


Why don't ThreeUI Pro components appear in package.json or npm update?


Pro components are not an npm dependency. The ThreeUI CLI copies their source straight into your project, so they are not listed in package.json, and npm update, npm outdated and lockfile-based dependency scanners do not track them. Treat Pro files as vendored code: keep them in version control and download them again with the CLI when you want a newer version.


Does every ThreeUI component need WebGL?


No. Some ThreeUI components draw with Canvas 2D instead of WebGL. The DesignCode preset of Brand Orbs, for example, draws its dot field with Canvas 2D. A blanket WebGL check hides these components from browsers that could run them, so apply a getContext('webgl2') or getContext('webgl') guard only to components whose threeui.com page describes a Three.js or WebGL renderer.


